response_text:
Creating a REST API involves designing a web service that uses HTTP requests to perform operations on resources. Here’s a step-by-step guide using **Node.js with Express** (a popular choice):

---

### ✅ Step 1: Set Up Your Project
```bash
mkdir my-rest-api
cd my-rest-api
npm init -y
```

Install Express:
```bash
npm install express
```

---

### ✅ Step 2: Create the Basic Server (`server.js`)
```js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const PORT = 3000;

// Middleware to parse JSON bodies
app.use(express.json());

// Sample data (in real apps, use a database)
let users = [
  { id: 1, name: "Alice" },
  { id: 2, name: "Bob" }
];

// GET /api/users - Retrieve all users
app.get('/api/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json(users);
});

// GET /api/users/:id - Get a single user by ID
app.get('/api/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const user = users.find(u => u.id === parseInt(req.params.id));
  if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: "User not found" });
  res.json(user);
});

// POST /api/users - Create a new user
app.post('/api/users', (req, res) => {
  const { name } = req.body;
  if (!name) return res.status(400).json({ error: "Name is required" });

  const newUser = { id: users.length + 1, name };
  users.push(newUser);
  res.status(201).json(newUser); // 201 Created
});

// PUT /api/users/:id - Update a user
app.put('/api/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const user = users.find(u => u.id === parseInt(req.params.id));
  if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: "User not found" });

  user.name = req.body.name || user.name;
  res.json(user);
});

// DELETE /api/users/:id - Remove a user
app.delete('/api/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const index = users.findIndex(u => u.id === parseInt(req.params.id));
  if (index === -1) return res.status(404).json({ error: "User not found" });

  users.splice(index, 1);
  res.json({ message: "User deleted" });
});

// Start the server
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

---

### ✅ Step 3: Test Your API

Use **curl**, **Postman**, or **Swagger UI**:

- `GET http://localhost:3000/api/users` → Returns all users
- `POST http://localhost:3000/api/users` with body `{ "name": "Charlie" }`
- `GET http://localhost:3000/api/users/1`

---

### ✅ Optional Enhancements

| Feature | How to Add |
|-------|------------|
| **Database (e.g., MongoDB, PostgreSQL)** | Use Mongoose or Prisma |
| **Authentication** | Add JWT or OAuth |
| **Validation** | Use `express-validator` |
| **Error Handling** | Centralize error middleware |
| **Documentation** | Use Swagger/OpenAPI |

---

### ✅ Best Practices

- Use **HTTP verbs correctly**: GET (read), POST (create), PUT/PATCH (update), DELETE.
- Return proper status codes: 200, 201, 400, 404, etc.
- Version your API (`/api/v1/users`)
- Use JSON for data exchange
- Secure with CORS, rate limiting, and input validation

---

✅ You now have a working REST API!  
👉 Next: Deploy it (e.g., to **Vercel**, **Render**, or **AWS**).

